We introduced a non-trivial unknotting operation in the domain of welded links, which stem from traditional links by permitting an additional "welding" operation that links a link's loopback to itself. We demonstrated that this surgery encompasses the surgery across all non-planar planar diagrams and serves as the planned unknotting move for all but one state model of the Kauffman invariant. Although we did not demonstrate that the move is unknotting for weaved or graph embeddings of unknotted spheres in $\mathbb{R}^3$, we were able to show that it is. We offered specific examples of its application to various complicated knotted structures, including Platonic and Archimedean knots, as well as other well-known knots such as the Stevedore, Tsudzuki, and various others. For these knots, we presented complete sequences of moves to transform them into the unknot.

